## Overview

miot-bridge-api is an HTTP/UDP/MQTT gateway between [Loxone](https://www.loxone.com/) and other home-automation controllers and Xiaomi devices that speak the [MIoT binary protocol](https://github.com/OpenMiHome/mihome-binary-protocol/blob/master/doc/PROTOCOL.md).

- **Register** devices once; the bridge caches the MIoT spec and manages the stamp/handshake lifecycle automatically.
- **Send commands** (read property, write property, execute action) via HTTP, UDP, or MQTT using a unified payload.
- **Subscribe** to device property changes; the bridge polls the device and pushes change events out to HTTP, UDP, or MQTT targets.

Swagger UI is available at the root path (`/`) once the server is running.

## Quick Start

### Docker Compose

Create a `config/production.json` (see [Configuration](#configuration)), then:

```yaml
services:
  miot-bridge-api:
    image: ghcr.io/radoslavirha/miot-bridge-api:latest
    ports:
      - "4001:4001"       # HTTP API + Swagger UI
      - "4002:4002/udp"   # UDP command listener (when udp.enabled = true)
    volumes:
      - ./config:/home/app/config:ro
      - ./cache:/home/app/cache     # only needed when mongodb.enabled = false
    restart: unless-stopped
```

> The config volume mounts your local `config/` directory to `/home/app/config` inside the container. Only `production.json` is required.

## Configuration

Configuration is loaded from `config/production.json`.

### Minimal configuration

```json
{
  "server": {
    "httpPort": 4001
  }
}
```

### Full configuration reference

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `server.httpPort` | number | ✅ | — | HTTP server port. |
| `cachePath` | string | | `./cache` | Path (relative to CWD) for JSON device and notification cache files. Used when MongoDB is disabled. |
| `mongodb.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Enable MongoDB as the device/notification storage backend. |
| `mongodb.url` | string | when enabled | — | MongoDB connection string, e.g. `mongodb://host:27017/miot-bridge-api`. |
| `mongodb.connectionOptions.user` | string | | — | MongoDB username. |
| `mongodb.connectionOptions.pass` | string | | — | MongoDB password. |
| `udp.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Enable UDP command listener. |
| `udp.port` | number | when enabled | — | UDP port to listen on. |
| `udp.notifications.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Send property-change notifications via UDP datagram. |
| `udp.notifications.address` | string | when enabled | — | Target `host:port`, e.g. `XXX:5000`. |
| `polling.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Enable periodic device property polling. |
| `polling.intervalMs` | number | | `15000` | Polling interval in milliseconds. |
| `polling.dispatchOnChange` | boolean | | `true` | Dispatch a notification only when the value changes. Set to `false` to dispatch on every cycle. |
| `polling.maxErrorCount` | number | | `3` | Consecutive device failures before the device is placed in back-off. |
| `polling.errorSkipCycles` | number | | `20` | Polling cycles to skip for a back-off device before retrying. |
| `http.notifications.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Send property-change notifications via HTTP POST. |
| `http.notifications.address` | string | when enabled | — | Target URL, e.g. `http://XXX:5001/notify`. |
| `mqtt.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Enable MQTT client (inbound commands + outbound notifications). |
| `mqtt.url` | string | when enabled | — | Broker URL, e.g. `mqtt://localhost:1883` or `mqtts://broker.example.com:8883`. |
| `mqtt.clientId` | string | | auto | MQTT client identifier. |
| `mqtt.username` | string | when enabled | — | Broker username (EMQX: API Key). |
| `mqtt.password` | string | when enabled | — | Broker password (EMQX: Secret Key). |
| `mqtt.topicPrefix` | string | | — | Optional prefix prepended to all topics, e.g. `home/office` → `home/office/miot-bridge-api/command`. |
| `mqtt.notifications.enabled` | boolean | | `false` | Send property-change notifications via MQTT. |

### Example: HTTP-only with file cache

```json
{
  "server": { "httpPort": 4001 },
  "cachePath": "./cache",
  "polling": {
    "enabled": true,
    "intervalMs": 10000,
    "dispatchOnChange": true
  },
  "http": {
    "notifications": {
      "enabled": true,
      "address": "http://192.168.1.140:5001/notify"
    }
  }
}
```

### Example: Full stack (MQTT broker + MongoDB)

```json
{
  "server": { "httpPort": 4001 },
  "mongodb": {
    "enabled": true,
    "url": "mongodb://mongo:27017/miot-bridge-api",
    "connectionOptions": { "user": "miot-bridge-api", "pass": "secret" }
  },
  "udp": { "enabled": true, "port": 4002 },
  "polling": { "enabled": true, "intervalMs": 15000, "dispatchOnChange": true },
  "mqtt": {
    "enabled": true,
    "url": "mqtt://mosquitto:1883",
    "username": "miot-bridge-api",
    "password": "secret",
    "notifications": { "enabled": true }
  }
}
```

## REST API

Swagger UI is served at `http://<host>:<httpPort>/`.

All responses use `application/json`. All request bodies must be `Content-Type: application/json`.

> **Device IDs**: Two different IDs appear in this API.
> - **App UUID** (`deviceId` path parameter) — a UUID assigned by miot-bridge-api when a device is registered. Used in device management and notification endpoints.
> - **Hardware ID** (`deviceId` in the command body) — the numeric MIoT device ID returned from the handshake (e.g. `XXX`). Used in the command endpoint only.

### Devices

#### `POST /devices/discover`

Performs a handshake with a device and returns its capabilities via MIoT spec. **Does not persist anything.**

**Request body**

```json
{
  "address": "XXX",
  "token": "XXX",
  "model": "xiaomi.vacuum.c102gl"
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `address` | string | Device IP address on the local network. |
| `token` | string | 32-char hex device token (from Xiaomi Cloud Tokens Extractor). |
| `model` | string | Device model identifier, e.g. `xiaomi.vacuum.c102gl`. |

**Response** `200` — device capabilities (simplified MIoT spec).

---

#### `POST /devices`

Registers a device: performs handshake, fetches MIoT spec, persists to cache.

**Request body** — same fields as `/devices/discover`.

**Response** `201` — registered device with app UUID and simplified MIoT spec.

---

#### `GET /devices`

Returns all registered devices.

**Response** `200`

```json
{
  "devices": [ { "..." : "..." } ]
}
```

---

#### `GET /devices/:deviceId`

Returns a single registered device by its app UUID.

**Response** `200` — device object.

---

#### `DELETE /devices/:deviceId`

Deletes a registered device from the cache.

**Response** `204 No Content`

---

### Notifications (property subscriptions)

#### `POST /devices/:deviceId/notifications`

Subscribes to one or more device properties. The poller reads these on every interval and dispatches change events.

**Request body**

```json
{
  "properties": ["vacuum:status", "battery:battery-level"]
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `properties` | string[] | MIoT spec property keys to subscribe to. |

**Response** `201` — list of created notification subscriptions.

---

#### `GET /devices/:deviceId/notifications`

Returns all active notification subscriptions for the device.

**Response** `200`

```json
{
  "notifications": [
    { "id": "uuid", "deviceId": "app-uuid", "property": "vacuum:status" }
  ]
}
```

---

#### `DELETE /devices/:deviceId/notifications`

Deletes **all** notification subscriptions for the device.

**Response** `204 No Content`

---

#### `DELETE /devices/:deviceId/notifications/:notificationId`

Deletes a single notification subscription.

**Response** `204 No Content`

---

### Command

#### `POST /command`

Sends a command (read property, write property, or execute action) to a registered device. Works identically over HTTP, UDP, and MQTT.

**Request body**

```json
{
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "command": "vacuum:start-sweep",
  "operation": "Action"
}
```

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `deviceId` | number | ✅ | Numeric MIoT hardware device ID (returned from registration). |
| `command` | string | ✅ | MIoT spec key, format `<service>:<property-or-action>`, e.g. `vacuum:status`, `fan-control:fan-level`. |
| `operation` | string | ✅ | One of `GetProperty`, `SetProperty`, `Action`. |
| `value` | number | for SetProperty | Value to set (must conform to property type and allowed values in the MIoT spec). |

**Response** `200`

```json
{
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "command": "vacuum:status",
  "operation": "GetProperty",
  "success": true,
  "value": 3
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `deviceId` | number | Hardware device ID the command was sent to. |
| `command` | string | Command key that was executed. |
| `operation` | string | Operation type that was performed. |
| `success` | boolean | Whether the command succeeded. |
| `value` | any | Returned value for `GetProperty` operations. |
| `error` | string | Error message when `success` is `false`. |

## MQTT Protocol

When `mqtt.enabled = true`, the bridge connects to the configured broker and subscribes to the command topic.

### Topics

| Topic | Direction | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `miot-bridge-api/command` | inbound | Publish a command from your controller. |
| `miot-bridge-api/response` | outbound | Bridge publishes the command response. |
| `miot-bridge-api/notifications` | outbound | Bridge publishes property-change events (when `mqtt.notifications.enabled = true`). |

With `mqtt.topicPrefix = "home/office"` all topics become `home/office/miot-bridge-api/command`, etc.

### Command payload

Same JSON shape as the HTTP `POST /command` body:

```json
{
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "command": "vacuum:start-sweep",
  "operation": "Action"
}
```

### Response payload

Same JSON shape as the HTTP `POST /command` response:

```json
{
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "command": "vacuum:start-sweep",
  "operation": "Action",
  "success": true
}
```

### Notification payload

```json
{
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "property": "vacuum:status",
  "value": 3
}
```

## UDP Protocol

When `udp.enabled = true`, the bridge binds a UDP socket on `udp.port`.

The UDP command payload adds a `version` discriminator field to the standard command shape:

```json
{
  "version": "v1",
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "command": "vacuum:status",
  "operation": "GetProperty"
}
```

The response is sent back to the sender's address and port in the same JSON shape as the HTTP command response.

## Notification Payload

All outbound notification transports (HTTP POST, UDP datagram, MQTT publish) use the same JSON payload:

```json
{
  "deviceId": XXX,
  "property": "vacuum:status",
  "value": 3
}
```

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `deviceId` | number | Hardware MIoT device ID of the reporting device. |
| `property` | string | MIoT spec property key that changed. |
| `value` | any | Current property value. |

## Releases

Releases are based on [Semantic Versioning][semver], and use the format
of ``MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH``. In a nutshell, the version will be incremented
based on the following:

- ``MAJOR``: Incompatible or major changes.
- ``MINOR``: Backwards-compatible new features and enhancements.
- ``PATCH``: Backwards-compatible bugfixes and package updates.

